<div class="header-banner">
    <h1>Lesson Planner – Theme # The Vessels Are Big Because... – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<h2>SECTION 2 — LESSON IDENTIFICATION TABLE</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td><b>Scenario:</b> Visiting the Panama Canal</td>
        <td><b>Theme:</b> The Vessels Are Big Because...</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td colspan="2"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b><br>
            Reading: reading1: Create lists from information read in texts.<br>
            reading2: Identify the main ideas and details in short narratives.<br>
            phonemicAwareness: Read regularly spelled one-syllable or two-syllable words represented by single letters.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b><br>
            Reading: Can create lists based on information read in texts about the Panama Canal (e.g., 'List five facts about the Panama Canal. For example, it connects two oceans.').<br>
            Can identify main ideas and supporting details in texts about the Panama Canal (e.g., 'Main idea: Vessels pass through the canal.' 'Detail: They transport goods and passengers.').<br>
            Can read and spell one-syllable or two-syllable words related to familiar concepts (e.g., 'vessel' and 'wave').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2>SECTION 3 — AOA STAGES TABLE</h2>
<h3>The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h3>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</td>
            <td>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a large picture of the Panama Canal with various vessels (ships, boats, yachts) passing through the locks. Students look at the picture. The teacher asks, "What do you see?" and guides students to identify familiar vocabulary from Lesson 1 (e.g., "I see a big ship!", "A boat!", "The canal!"). The teacher elicits responses about size: "Is the ship big or small?" to connect to the theme "The Vessels Are Big Because...".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher points to a simple sentence on the board: "The Panama Canal connects two oceans." and reads it aloud slowly, pointing to each word. The teacher models the pronunciation of key words like 'canal' /kænˈæl/ and 'boat' /boʊt/, emphasizing the /k/ and /b/ sounds. The teacher then models reading a short descriptive sentence: "This vessel is bigger than that yacht." The teacher also models blending sounds for words like 'boat' (/b/ /oʊt/).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does the Panama Canal connect oceans or rivers?" (Expected answer: Oceans.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'vessel' a small boat or a big ship?" (Expected answer: It can be both, but here we mean big ship/boat.)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td>___ / ___ <br> (5-7 min)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Visiting the Panama Canal")</td>
            <td>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short narrative excerpt titled "A Trip to the Canal" (printed on a worksheet or projected). The text is simple and uses present simple and present continuous sentences.
                <br>Example Text:
                "Today, we are visiting the Panama Canal. It is very important and historic. We see many big vessels. A large ship is crossing the locks right now. It transports goods. That vessel is bigger than the small yacht. The Panama Canal connects the Atlantic and Pacific Oceans. Many people observe the vessels traveling through the wide water current. It is fascinating!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing facts (e.g., 'The Panama Canal connects two oceans.') and describing current events (e.g., 'They are watching the vessels cross the canal.').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> The teacher asks, "What is this text about?" (Expected answer: Visiting the Panama Canal.) Students quickly scan to get the main idea.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Activity:</b> Students read the text again and answer True or False questions.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The Panama Canal connects two rivers." (False)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Many small yachts are crossing the locks now." (False - a large ship is mentioned, a yacht is smaller)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The canal is important and historic." (True)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td>___ / ___ <br> (10-12 min)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>(Accuracy focus on Reading)</td>
            <td>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading to identify specific information and understand sentence structure. Focusing on present simple and present continuous, comparatives (big/bigger), and vocabulary (canal, vessel, ship, locks, connects, travels, goods). Phonemic awareness for spelling one-syllable or two-syllable words (e.g., 'canal', 'vessel', 'wave', 'boat').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – Fill-in-the-blanks:</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with sentences from the "A Trip to the Canal" text, but with key vocabulary words missing. Students read the sentences and choose the correct word from a word bank (e.g., 'canal', 'vessels', 'connects', 'bigger', 'goods', 'locks') to complete them. This reinforces reading for details and specific words.
                        <br>Example: "The Panama ___ two oceans." (Word bank: connects, canal, sees)
                        <br>Example: "A ship is crossing the ___ right now." (Word bank: goods, locks, ocean)
                        <br>The teacher checks answers as a class, encouraging students to read the completed sentences aloud.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – Sentence Matching:</b> The teacher provides two columns. Column A has main ideas or sentence beginnings from the text, and Column B has supporting details or sentence endings. Students read both columns and draw lines to match them correctly. This practices identifying main ideas and details.
                        <br>Example Column A: "The Panama Canal..." / "A large ship is..."
                        <br>Example Column B: "...crossing the locks." / "...connects two oceans."
                        <br>This also includes reading and spelling focus for 'canal', 'vessel', 'wave', 'boat' by having them present in the sentences.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – Read and Spell:</b> In pairs, students take turns reading out one-syllable or two-syllable words from a list related to the scenario (e.g., 'canal', 'vessel', 'wave', 'boat', 'ocean', 'goods', 'wide', 'deep', 'big', 'small'). Their partner listens and tries to spell the word aloud. The teacher circulates, observing their reading and spelling, and providing immediate feedback on pronunciation (especially /k/ and /b/ sounds) and correct spelling using alphabet knowledge.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td>___ / ___ <br> (15-18 min)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Visiting the Panama Canal")</td>
            <td>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group Reading and List Creation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students). The teacher provides each group with a new, slightly longer but still A1.2 level, narrative text about a different aspect of the Panama Canal (e.g., "Different Types of Vessels at the Canal" or "Life at the Locks"). The text will incorporate more instances of comparatives and superlatives, present simple, and present continuous, along with target vocabulary (vessel, ship, boat, yacht, goods, travel, connect, big, bigger, biggest, small, smaller, smallest, locks, ocean).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group reads the text together quietly first.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Then, they are given two tasks to complete by reading the text again and discussing:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Task 1 (Create a list):</b> "List three facts about the vessels or the canal from the text. For example, 'It connects two oceans.'" Students write down their list. (Reading: reading1)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Task 2 (Identify main idea and details):</b> "What is the main idea of this text? And what are two important details?" Students write down their main idea and details. (Reading: reading2)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher encourages them to use polite language when sharing opinions and inviting others to participate (Sociolinguistic: 'What do you think?', 'I think the main idea is...').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups then share their lists and main ideas/details with the class, reading directly from their notes or the text. The teacher facilitates, correcting errors gently and reinforcing target vocabulary and grammar.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td>___ / ___ <br> (10-12 min)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>(Formative assessment strategy)</td>
            <td>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on students' ability to extract information from a text, identify main ideas, and create lists. Peer assessment during group work (students check each other's lists). </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students receive a short, new paragraph (3-4 sentences) related to the Panama Canal.
                <br>Example: "The Miraflores Locks are a busy place. Many tourists visit every day to see the big ships. These ships are transporting important goods across the ocean. The biggest ship today is blue and carries cars."
                <br>Students individually read the paragraph and complete two sentences:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One fact from the text is: ________________________"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The main idea of this text is: ________________________"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in identifying facts and the main idea from the text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrated comprehension of target vocabulary (e.g., 'ships', 'goods', 'locks', 'visit', 'biggest').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to read and understand simple sentences independently.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new word you read today about the Panama Canal?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you now find facts in a text? Yes / No / A little bit"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td>___ / ___ <br> (5-7 min)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></td>
            <td>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy for you when reading about the Panama Canal today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of reading or understanding the text was a little difficult?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b> The teacher reflects on the effectiveness of the reading activities. Did students successfully create lists and identify main ideas and details from the texts? Were the texts at the appropriate A1.2 level? Was the pronunciation practice helpful for 'canal' and 'boat'? What adjustments are needed for reading comprehension in future lessons (e.g., more visual support, chunking text into smaller parts, additional vocabulary pre-teaching)? Were the comparative and superlative structures clear to students in the reading context? How well did they engage in respectful conversations during group work?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The content learned today, especially the facts and details about the Panama Canal and its vessels, will be used as a foundation for "Speaking & Interaction" in Lesson 3. Students will use the information they read today to describe vessels, compare them, and discuss facts about the canal with their classmates.</p>
            </td>
            <td>___ / ___ <br> (3-5 min)</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>